 [F3] Closed bounded subsets of Euclidean space are compact ([[thm-heine-borel-rn]]).
 
+[F4] The exponential is smooth with derivative equal to itself ([[thm-derivative-of-exponential]]), and $e^u>0$, $e^{-u}=1/e^u$ for every real $u$ ([[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]]).
+
+[F5] The scalar sum, product and quotient rules hold; in particular $(1/g)'=-g'/g^2$ wherever $g\ne0$ ([[thm-algebra-of-derivatives]]).
+
+[F6] Sums and products of continuous scalar functions are continuous, as are quotients on the domain of a nonvanishing denominator ([[thm-algebra-of-continuous-functions]]).
+
 ## Proof
 
 **Proof technique:** direct.
 
-1.1 Define $a(t)=e^{-1/t}$ for $t>0$ and $a(t)=0$ for $t\le0$. If $s=1/t$, induction gives $a^{(k)}(t)=P_k(s)e^{-s}$ on $t>0$, with $P_0=1$ and $P_{k+1}(s)=s^2(P_k(s)-P_k'(s))$. By [F1], both $P_k(s)e^{-s}$ and $sP_k(s)e^{-s}$ tend to zero as $s\to\infty$. Extending each derivative by zero to $t\le0$ is therefore continuous; its difference quotient at zero also tends to zero. Induction proves $a\in C^\infty(\mathbb R)$ with every derivative zero at zero. [F1, F2, algebra]
+1.1 Define $a(t)=e^{-1/t}$ for $t>0$ and $a(t)=0$ for $t\le0$. On $t>0$, put $s=1/t$. The quotient rule [F5] applied to $1/t$ gives $s'=-1/t^2=-s^2$, while [F2] and [F4] give $(e^{-s})'=-e^{-s}$ as a function of $s$. Thus the product and chain rules give
+$$\frac{d}{dt}\bigl(P_k(s)e^{-s}\bigr)=\bigl(P_k'(s)-P_k(s)\bigr)e^{-s}(-s^2)=s^2\bigl(P_k(s)-P_k'(s)\bigr)e^{-s}.$$
+Starting at $P_0=1$, this proves $a^{(k)}(t)=P_k(s)e^{-s}$ with the polynomial recurrence $P_{k+1}(s)=s^2(P_k(s)-P_k'(s))$. By [F1], both $P_k(s)e^{-s}$ and $sP_k(s)e^{-s}$ tend to zero as $s\to\infty$. Extending each derivative by zero to $t\le0$ is therefore continuous; its difference quotient at zero also tends to zero. Induction proves $a\in C^\infty(\mathbb R)$ with every derivative zero at zero. [F1, F2, F4, F5, F6, algebra, induction]
 
-2.1 Set $\sigma(t)=a(t)/(a(t)+a(1-t))$. For $t\le0$, the second summand in the denominator is positive; for $t\ge1$, the first is positive; for $0<t<1$, both are positive. Thus the quotient is smooth, $0\le\sigma\le1$, and $\sigma=0$ on $t\le0$, $\sigma=1$ on $t\ge1$. Define $\chi(x)=\sigma((4-|x|^2)/3)$. Repeated [F2] proves smoothness, and the two constant regions give the claimed unit and zero regions, including their boundaries. Its closed support lies in the closed radius-two ball and is compact by [F3]. [step 1.1, F2, F3]
+2.1 Set $\sigma(t)=a(t)/(a(t)+a(1-t))$. For $t\le0$, the second summand in the denominator is positive; for $t\ge1$, the first is positive; for $0<t<1$, both are positive. Write $g(t)=a(t)+a(1-t)$, which is smooth by step 1.1 and the sum and chain rules. Since $g>0$, [F6] makes $q=1/g$ continuous, and [F5] gives $q'=-g'q^2$. For every $k\ge0$, finite iteration of the product rule [F5] and continuity of products [F6] shows that products of $C^k$ functions are $C^k$. If $q\in C^k$, the displayed derivative is therefore $C^k$ because $g'$ is smooth; hence $q\in C^{k+1}$. Starting with $C^0$, induction proves $q\in C^\infty$ and then $\sigma=aq\in C^\infty$. Thus $0\le\sigma\le1$,  and $\sigma=0$ on $t\le0$, $\sigma=1$ on $t\ge1$. Define $\chi(x)=\sigma((4-|x|^2)/3)$. Repeated [F2] proves smoothness, and the two constant regions give the claimed unit and zero regions, including their boundaries. Its closed support lies in the closed radius-two ball and is compact by [F3]. [step 1.1, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, induction]
 
